<h2>When to See a Doctor About Hair Loss</h2>
<div class="alert alert-warning mt-3 mb-4 shadow-sm border-0" style="background-color: #fff3cd; border-left: 5px solid #ffc107 !important;">
<p class="mb-2"><strong>Get your hair loss checked if:</strong></p>
<ul class="mb-0">
<li>Hair falls out suddenly, in clumps, or leaves round bald patches</li>
<li>The scalp is red, scaly, painful, itchy, or has pus or boils (in children, a fungal infection is a common cause)</li>
<li>You also lose eyebrow, eyelash or body hair</li>
<li>Hair loss comes with tiredness, weight change, feeling cold, irregular periods or acne</li>
<li>Shedding has lasted more than six months</li>
<li>A child is losing hair</li>
</ul>
</div>
<p>A doctor will usually examine the scalp and may suggest blood tests such as a complete blood count, ferritin (iron stores), thyroid function (TSH), vitamin D and vitamin B12, and hormone tests for some women. Scalp conditions are best assessed by a dermatologist.</p>

